From: Bogdan Mircea <mirceapetrebogdan@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] pppd: bind CCP retry identifiers to receive state

ccp_addci() allocates kernel receive state while building each request,
including timer retries. A queued Ack for the preceding allocation can
otherwise open CCP after that receiver was replaced. Linux then ignores
the current Ack because CCP is already up, leaving its receiver stopped.

Use the native retransmit callback to advance the request identifier before
rebuilding receive state. The existing FSM reply checks reject stale IDs;
its retry budget and timeout remain unchanged. Initial requests, peer reply
identifiers, Reset-Request handling and other control protocols retain
their owners. This also covers the other CCP receive-compression methods;
it does not change encryption keys or MTU handling.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Bogdan Mircea <mirceapetrebogdan@gmail.com>

--- a/pppd/ccp.c
+++ b/pppd/ccp.c
@@ -216,6 +216,7 @@ static int mppe_mtu[NUM_PPP];
 static void ccp_resetci (fsm *);
 static int  ccp_cilen (fsm *);
 static void ccp_addci (fsm *, u_char *, int *);
+static void ccp_retransmit (fsm *);
 static int  ccp_ackci (fsm *, u_char *, int);
 static int  ccp_nakci (fsm *, u_char *, int, int);
 static int  ccp_rejci (fsm *, u_char *, int);
@@ -239,7 +240,7 @@ static fsm_callbacks ccp_callbacks = {
     NULL,
     NULL,
     NULL,
-    NULL,
+    ccp_retransmit,
     ccp_extcode,
     "CCP"
 };
@@ -708,6 +709,18 @@ static int
 }
 
 /*
+ * A retry rebuilds the kernel receive state in ccp_addci().  Give it a new
+ * identifier so a queued Ack for the retired state cannot open CCP before
+ * the replacement has been initialized by its own Ack.  RFC 1661 permits
+ * changing the identifier on retransmission; the FSM keeps its retry budget.
+ */
+static void
+ccp_retransmit(fsm *f)
+{
+    f->reqid = ++f->id;
+}
+
+/*
  * ccp_addci - put our requests in a packet.
  */
 static void
